00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.739
[INTRO]

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:40.090
The Shining is my favorite movie of all time,
and it came out 39 years ago in May of 1980.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.050
The prologue of Doctor Sleep, where Rose and
her cult eat the steam of the little girl,

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:48.140
Violet, takes place in Florida in 1980, which
is where Halloran was during the Overlook

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.740
Hotel's off-season in the Shining.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:51.480
"Dick!

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.420
How you doing?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.180
How's the weather down there?"

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.760
"I'm not in Florida, Larry.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.260
I'm calling from the Stapleton Airport."

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.360
"What the hell you doing down there?"

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.440
"Well I just got in from Miami and I gotta
get up to the Overlook today."

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.759
Rose and her gang have the ability to sense
the biggest shiners in the country and go

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:07.759
after them.

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:10.840
Is it possible that they came to Florida to
get Halloran, when they heard him trying to

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.209
reach Danny at the Overlook Hotel, but missed
him and took Violet as a consolation prize?

00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:20.049
Another homage to the year 1980 is seen during
the exterior of Abra's house, the street number

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:21.099
is 1980.

00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:24.979
Doctor Sleep features a musical score that
is very similar to that of The Shining, where

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:27.990
director Stanley Kubrick used almost all Classical
Music.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.109
From the eerie haunting build of Bela Bartok's
"Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta"

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:35.749
used when Danny and Jack have the one on one
talk in the hotel room being referenced in

00:01:35.749 --> 00:01:38.460
the music that we hear early on in the trailer
community,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.740
(Music)

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.340
...to the big pulsating tribal drums that
echoed through the massive empty rooms of

00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:56.960
the Overlook following Danny to Florida, New
Jersey, to the middle of nowhere and back

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:57.960
Colorado.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.479
But the Shining also features a couple of
Jazz pieces, which can be heard when the hotel

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:05.630
is showing off events of days past, like Henry
Hall's Home which celebrates Jack Torrance's

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.950
homecoming to the Gold Ballroom, and Al Bowlly
and Ray Noble's "It's All Forgotten Now",

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.910
which plays while Grady tries to get Jack
to remember his role at the Overlook, to correct

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.500
his family to protect the hotel.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.680
"But I... corrected... them sir.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:31.920
And when my wife tried to prevent me from
doing my duty, I... corrected... her."

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.530
These songs can be heard in Doctor Sleep on
the nomads' radio while they camp out and

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.511
try to tune into the mind of a Shiner with
a lot of steam for them to capture next, which

00:02:39.511 --> 00:02:43.480
reminded me of the line from Abra's mom where
she says, "I know that head of yours is like

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.480
a radio."

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.000
So perhaps Rose and her gang are also picking
up on some evil signals broadcasted from The

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Overlook.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.760
It's during these Dream hunting sequences,
that Doctor Sleep also references a concept

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.140
seen elsewhere in the writings of Stephen
King.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.349
Rose invades the mind of Abra while she is
asleep and sees a wall covered in filing cabinets

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:02.599
with memories and important facts about her
life.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:07.299
King's 2001 novel Dreamcatcher gives us a
look into the mind of the character Jonesy,

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:11.300
who similarly files his memories away to a
warehouse, resembling a building in Derry

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.519
called Tracker Bros shipping, and it too becomes
the victim of an invasion, but Abra's mind

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:16.709
in Doctor Sleep is far more prepared to defend
itself.

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:20.069
Of course, most of the connections to King’s
writing in this movie are going to be connections

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:23.590
to the source material, Doctor Sleep and it’s
prequel, The Shining.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.139
For many movie fans, when they think of the
most cursed room at The Overlook, they think

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:32.340
of Room 237, but the infamous room number
was changed from Room 217 in the novel.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.390
This is because of a request made by the hotel
where the exteriors were filmed, The Timberline

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:37.390
Lodge.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.670
The hotel, in perhaps one of the worst marketing
blunders of all time, feared that people would

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.249
be too scared to stay in Room 217.

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:46.680
Doctor Sleep makes a little homage to the
original room number when Danny Torrance first

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.609
starts working at the hospice in New Jersey.

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:52.599
He sees the cat, Azzy, wander into the room
of a dying patient, the first instance of

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:56.779
him earning his nickname, Doctor Sleep, and
the room number on the door is 217.

00:03:56.779 --> 00:04:01.489
There’s also the fact that Danny, like his
father Jack before him, has become an alcoholic.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:05.879
Jack’s alcoholism is present in Kubrick’s
adaptation, but is focused on much more in

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:09.659
the novel, where Jack has a catch phrase associated
with his drunken beatings of Danny,

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:11.439
“Come on and take your medicine!

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.040
Take it like a man!”

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.300
Now despite this line never appearing in the 1980
film, it does make a comeback at the end of

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:21.630
Doctor Sleep, where Danny comes face to face
one more time with his father at the bar in

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:22.630
the Gold Room.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.110
Uhh… they do know that Jack Nicholson is
still alive right?

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.770
He also pours a glass of Jack Daniels, which
is symbolic of the fact that both Jack and

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:31.979
Dan resorted to alcoholism at some point.

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:36.169
But the Overlook itself would not be the only
major connection to Stanley Kubrick’s psychological

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:39.780
horror masterpiece, when we return, I’ll
have even more hidden connections featured

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:40.640
in Doctor Sleep.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.740
One scene I found particularly interesting
in Doctor Sleep was right after Rose went

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.180
into Abra’s mind and saw the inside of her
room.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:55.479
Abra decides to rearrange her furniture and
make it look like a different room to protect

00:04:55.479 --> 00:04:56.479
her privacy.

